<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Portuguese rounding of the Cape of Good Hope</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Portuguese rounding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a> refers to the voyages that opened a sea route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> around southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, first achieved by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolomeu-dias">Bartolomeu Dias</a> in 1488 and followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a>'s voyage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The route allowed Portugal to trade directly in Asian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>. It began redirecting spice commerce away from routes controlled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p></div>
